7] [17592186516762 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516762 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516762 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4eeffe0-d387-54ea-99bf-7c07fda39ad8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. Stretching with stretch factor $6$ in the $x$-direction, then a translation by $2$ units downward\n\nII. Rotation by ${45}^{∘}$ clockwise around a point $O$ followed by reflection over a line $l$\n\nIII. Dilation of scale factor $4$ with center at $A,$ then translation by $6$ units to the right" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ea750dd-05b5-599b-a75b-1d40366d85dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516783 #edb/ref 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I includes stretching in only one direction. Thus, it is not a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a combination of rotation and a reflection, which are rigid motions. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of dilation of scale factor $4$ and a translation. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. $(x,y)↦(x-5,y)$\n\nII. $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\n\nIII. $(x,y)↦(x,3y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cfe4ea96-fddc-550c-ac7a-6ec2d2c94652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516855 #edb/ref 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations. \n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I represents a translation by $5$ units to the left. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II represents a rotation by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise around the origin. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III represents a stretch of factor $3$ in the $y$-direction. This is not a rigid motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1071 "A square $ABCD$ is translated by $3$ units to the left and $3$ units upward. If the resulting quadrilateral is $PQRS,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $ABCD∼PQRS$\n\nII. $ABCD≅PQRS$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{BC}≅\\overset{―}{QR}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8890925-6102-5a39-922f-cbb90bd120c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516862 #edb/ref 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations. \n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are given a combination of translations. As a result, we obtain that \n\n$$\nABCD∼PQRS.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since our combined transformation consists of rigid motions only (namely, translations), the distance between all points is preserved under the transformation. Therefore,  we obtain that \n\n$$\nABCD≅PQRS.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $ABCD≅PQRS,$ all corresponding sides must be congruent. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC}≅\\overset{―}{QR}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34047e62-3d93-591d-8a51-e16282e2c37f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516967 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516967 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccd3e0af-ff85-5d17-8152-0c5d9758036f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516967 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516979 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516979 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab95375-eb28-5e00-b681-d227e2c00467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516979 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516855 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
